      en: "Bus stops and similar areas have unpredictable pedestrian movement — people can step off the kerb suddenly to board or after leaving a bus. The safe, textbook response is to slow down and watch for any indication (a glance, a step toward the kerb, a wave) that a pedestrian is about to enter the road, so you have time to stop or react. Speeding up (option 1) removes your reaction time and directly increases crash risk. High beam (option 2) is irrelevant in daytime pedestrian-heavy areas and is otherwise reserved for low-light conditions with no other road users nearby — it does nothing to protect pedestrians and can even dazzle other drivers.",
