      en: "A correct, relaxed riding position is the foundation of good motorcycle control: it lets the rider's arms and shoulders stay loose so the bike can steer naturally, absorbs bumps through the legs and core instead of the handlebars, and keeps the rider ready to react quickly to hazards. Sitting as far back as possible does not meaningfully increase rear-tyre grip and can hamper control of the front end, while gripping the handlebars tightly causes arm fatigue, fights the motorcycle's self-correcting steering geometry, and reduces the rider's feel for what the front tyre is doing — both are classic mistakes taught against in rider training.",
